          %p
            The reason for the
            = succeed "-terminology" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            of — the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            %em measurement basis
            and the phenomenon of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction+collapse"} wavefunction collapse
            entailed by the measurement process. It would in fact make good sense to speak of the
            %strong quantum measurement monad
            — but notice that the conceptual difference between “reading” (say a global parameter, as is common interpretation for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
            when used as a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} monad in computer science
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            of the quantum reader (its “
            %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#ModalQuantumLogic"}> quantum modal logic
            ”, if you wish).
          %p
            Often one is interested in the case that the parameter set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            here is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            (explicitly so in applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
            where eg.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bool"} Bool
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
            %em real
            measurement in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            can only ever resolve a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            of potential outcomes), and this is the case we shall focus on now unless otherwise specified.
          %p
            In this case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            parameter set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
